One of the things I enjoy the most about computer science is the amount of problem
solving needed to accomplish a specific task. Over the summer heading into 2026, I
spent time working for Malcolm, a startup which specializes in providing a platform
for small-scale funds to more easily track their data, I was able to solve problems
in a collaborative manner, which I found extremely rewarding. Additionally, due to being
actively involved with the Programming Team at my school, I was given the opportunity to
sit the British Informatics Olympiad and British Algorithmic Olympiad, scoring a respectable
50 marks in the Informatics Olympiad out of 98 and a Silver Certificate in the Algorithmic
Olympiad.

My interest in low-level programming started in Year 9, when I watched a
video of someone building their own operating system to run Tetris. I began to
research more in this area, and ultimately decided to build my own simple operating
system in Rust for my Duke of Edinburgh skills award. This allowed me to explore paging
and memory management algorithms, which eventually led me into building my
own C compiler in Rust as well.

Aside from low-level programming, I am also interested in computer architecture. Over
the summer heading into Year 10, I experimented with building components of a CPU in
Minecraft, which introduced me to ALU basics and allowed me to discover how different
parts of a computer interact with the CPU. At university, I hope to eventually
specialize in computer engineering, as the architecture of processors and the ways in
which optimizations are made to improve processor performance is a topic that I am
very interested in.



During the Computer Science GCSE, I was introduced to the different types of operating systems,
which I was encouraged to look into more by my teachers. I used this knowledge to
install a Linux distribution on a MacBook, which, in turn, allowed me to explore the
complexities of installing operating systems on devices such as MacBooks, which are
closed off and often have hardware security feature implemented. I am excited to
explore further the ways in which hardware is used to secure a system at university.

The Mathematics and Further Mathematics A-level has also been intriguing,
specifically the Additional Pure module I am taking, due to being in the top two
Further Mathematics sets. In the past, I have performed well in maths, getting gold
in the Intermediate Maths Challenge and silver in the Senior Maths Challenge, so I
was excited to begin the Additional Pure module. One of the first topics we started
was Number Theory and Modular Arithmetic, which has close ties to Discrete
Mathematics and is something I have found very enjoyable.

The Physics A-level has also been useful in developing my problem-solving
capabilities for more advanced problems. I attend Physics Extension regularly, where
we solve advanced physics and mechanics problems. When the sessions first started, I
struggled with figuring out the methods I needed to use to solve problems, especially
when multiple steps were needed to get the answer. However, as the sessions continued
I improved my thought process when it came to answering questions, and got better at
picking techniques to solve problems with.

